#!/bin/zsh -l
# Liveness channel for the careerforge nightly backup (M14-04 residual).
#
# The 02:00 job's only failure channel was an unread log, and six silent
# failures proved that is no channel at all. This check runs at 09:00 and
# alerts LOUDLY unless a fresh backup artifact exists on the share. "Cannot
# verify" - share unreachable, directory missing, permission denied - is an
# ALERT, never a skip: silence was the original defect.
#
# Named without an extension so launchd and the macOS "Login Items &
# Extensions" UI show "careerforge-backup-liveness" rather than a bare "zsh",
# matching its sibling careerforge-backup. The login shell (-l) sources the
# user profile so node is on PATH under launchd's minimal environment.
#
# Config comes from the repo's .env (read, never sourced; values never echoed).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE LISTING GOES THROUGH node (2026-08-14 architecture audit)
#
# This check false-alarmed every morning on 2026-08-13 and 2026-08-14, logging
# "NO backup artifacts found in the backup dir at all" while six healthy dumps
# sat in that directory. The backups were fine; the VERIFIER was broken, which
# is worse than no verifier - a daily false alarm trains the operator to ignore
# the one alert protecting data that has no other copy.
#
# Two diagnoses were tested and REJECTED before the real one was found:
#   (a) "the SMB share dropped" - disproven, the share was mounted throughout.
#   (b) "Program vs ProgramArguments in the plist" - disproven by a controlled
#       launchd probe running both forms; they failed identically.
#
# The measured cause: under a launchd agent, macOS TCC permits stat() but
# denies readdir() on a network volume. `[ -d ]` therefore PASSES and the
# listing returns "Operation not permitted", which the old code could not tell
# apart from an empty directory. Per-binary probe, same share, same minute:
#
#     node        -> OK (6 dumps)      <- holds the network-volume grant
#     zsh ls      -> DENIED
#     /bin/ls     -> DENIED
#     python3     -> DENIED (PermissionError)
#
# So the listing is delegated to node - the same binary `pnpm db:backup`
# already uses, which is exactly why the 02:00 job could always WRITE to the
# share while this 09:00 check could not READ it. No MDM/PPPC profile needed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT ADVERSARIAL REVIEW FOUND IN THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS FIX (2026-08-15)
#
# The three-state requirement was met and no condition had been weakened, but
# two SILENT FALSE GREENS survived, and a false green is the one outcome this
# script exists to prevent:
#
#   (a) FUTURE MTIME. The age comparison was `[ "$AGE" -ge "$MAX_AGE_HOURS" ]`
#       with no lower bound. An artifact dated in the future yields a NEGATIVE
#       age, which is less than the threshold, so the script logged OK and
#       exited 0 - permanently green. Clock skew on a NAS makes this ordinary,
#       not exotic. A negative age is now its own alert: it means something is
#       wrong with the clock on one side, and "I cannot trust these timestamps"
#       is exactly the state this script must never round down to OK.
#
#   (b) DELIMITER PARSING. The probe emitted `STATE|count|newest|age|err` and
#       the shell split it on "|". A "|" in a filename shifted every field, fed
#       a non-numeric value to `-ge`, and zsh's error left the `if` false - so
#       the script fell through to log OK. Fixed structurally rather than by
#       quoting: the probe now emits ONE FIELD PER LINE.
#
#       Precisely why that is safe, because an earlier version of this comment
#       got it wrong: newlines ARE legal in macOS filenames (tested - the
#       script handles one correctly). The protection is not the filesystem, it
#       is the probe's own matching: the family patterns use `.` and
#       non-multiline anchors, so a name containing a newline cannot match a
#       family in the first place and never reaches the emitted fields. Adding
#       an `s` or `m` flag to those regexes, or switching them to `includes()`,
#       would silently reintroduce field shifting.
#
# Two coverage gaps were also found and closed, both of them cases where the
# check would have stayed green through a real backup failure:
#
#   (c) ONLY THE DB DUMP WAS CHECKED. The nightly job writes three artifact
#       families; the profile tar - which the backup PR itself calls "data that
#       has no other copy" - could have stopped being produced entirely and
#       this check would have reported OK forever. Both families are now
#       checked independently, and either one going stale alerts.
#
#   (d) A ZERO-BYTE ARTIFACT COUNTED AS A BACKUP. A truncated or failed dump
#       that still created its file passed. Size is now asserted.
#
# The check distinguishes these outcomes and alerts on all but the first:
#   OK           fresh, non-empty artifact in every family
#   STALE        newest artifact in a family older than the threshold
#   FUTURE       newest artifact is dated ahead of now - clocks disagree
#   ZEROSIZE     newest artifact exists but is empty
#   EMPTY        readdir succeeded, a family has no artifacts at all
#   DENIED       readdir refused - CANNOT VERIFY (the state that used to lie)
#   UNREACHABLE  directory absent / share unmounted
#   ERROR        anything else, including an unparseable probe
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

REPO_ROOT="${0:A:h}/../.."
LOG="$HOME/Library/Logs/careerforge-backup-liveness.log"

envval() { grep -m1 "^$1=" "$REPO_ROOT/.env" 2>/dev/null | cut -d= -f2-; }

# printf, not echo: zsh's builtin `echo` EXPANDS backslash escapes, and these
# messages embed a filename read off the backup share. A file named
# `careerforge-db-2026\n<a forged OK line>\c.dump.age` injected a whole fake
# entry into this log and suppressed the trailing newline so the next real
# entry fused onto it - forging the very artifact RUNBOOKS tells the operator
# to read. Same attacker capability as the osascript injection below, against
# the evidence instead of the machine.
log()   { printf '%s %s\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z')" "$1" >> "$LOG"; }

# The message is passed as an ARGUMENT to osascript, never interpolated into
# the AppleScript source.
#
# This was a live command-injection path, found on the third adversarial pass
# and present since the first version of this file. Several alert messages
# embed $NEWEST - a filename read off the backup share - and the family regex
# allows a filename to contain a double quote. Interpolating it closed the
# AppleScript string literal and the rest of the name was evaluated as code.
# Reproduced exactly:
#
#   osascript -e 'set x to "ZERO BYTES (careerforge-db-2026" \
#     & (do shell script "echo INJECTED-AND-EXECUTED") & ".dump.age)"
#     return x'
#   -> ZERO BYTES (careerforge-db-2026INJECTED-AND-EXECUTED.dump.age)
#
# So anyone who could write a file to that SMB share got code execution as
# Carlos at 09:00, from a check whose entire job is to be trustworthy. The
# `on run {m}` form takes the text as data; the same payload now comes back
# as a literal string.
alert() {
  log "ALERT [$1]: $2"
  osascript -e 'on run {m}
display notification m with title "CareerForge backup ALERT" sound name "Basso"
end run' -- "$2"
  exit 1
}

# Threshold resolution order: process environment, then .env, then the default.
# RUNBOOKS documented this as configurable while the first version read the
# process environment ONLY - and launchd supplies no environment, so it was
# permanently 26 in production and an operator who set it in .env got silence.
# A key that is PRESENT but empty is an operator typo, not an absence, so it is
# refused rather than silently defaulted - "I set it and nothing happened" is
# the failure this whole file exists to stop.
# An empty value in the PROCESS environment is refused too, not just an empty
# one in .env. The first version of this only covered .env, so
# `BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS= ./check` silently fell back to 26 - the same
# "I set it and nothing happened" failure in the other half of the lookup.
if [ "${BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS+set}" = set ] && [ -z "${BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS}" ]; then
  alert ERROR "BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS is set to an empty value in the environment - refusing to guess a threshold"
fi

MAX_AGE_HOURS="${BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS-}"
if [ -z "$MAX_AGE_HOURS" ]; then
  if grep -q '^BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS=' "$REPO_ROOT/.env" 2>/dev/null; then
    MAX_AGE_HOURS="$(envval BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS)"
    [ -z "$MAX_AGE_HOURS" ] && alert ERROR "BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS is set to an empty value in .env - refusing to guess a threshold"
  else
    MAX_AGE_HOURS=26
  fi
fi
# Positive match, and >= 1. Zero would alert STALE every single morning on a
# perfectly healthy backup, which is the alert-fatigue outcome this file was
# written to end - reachable from a one-character typo.
# BOUNDED, not just numeric. `^[0-9]+$` alone admits a value zsh arithmetic
# cannot represent: at 19+ digits the comparison below emits "number truncated
# after 18 digits", evaluates FALSE, and control falls through to `log OK` -
# the original defect again, in the one place the value is operator-supplied.
# Six digits is 114 years of hours; nothing legitimate is longer.
[[ "$MAX_AGE_HOURS" =~ '^[0-9]{1,6}$' ]] \
  || alert ERROR "BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS ('$MAX_AGE_HOURS') is not a 1-6 digit positive integer - refusing to guess a threshold"
[ "$MAX_AGE_HOURS" -lt 1 ] \
  && alert ERROR "BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS is $MAX_AGE_HOURS - a threshold below 1 hour would alert every morning on a healthy backup"

BACKUP_DIR="$(envval BACKUP_DIR)"
[ -z "$BACKUP_DIR" ] && alert UNREACHABLE "BACKUP_DIR is not set in .env - the nightly job cannot be running"

# Same remount courtesy as the backup wrapper: a reboot drops the share, and a
# 09:00 false alarm every reboot morning would train the alert to be ignored.
SMB_URL="$(envval BACKUP_SMB_URL)"
SMB_MP="$(envval BACKUP_SMB_MOUNT_POINT)"
if [ -n "$SMB_URL" ] && [ -n "$SMB_MP" ] && ! mount | grep -qF " $SMB_MP (smbfs"; then
  # Argument form here too. This line was left interpolated when the alert
  # path was fixed, and it is the same defect: BACKUP_SMB_URL comes from .env
  # via `envval`, which does no quote handling, so a URL containing a double
  # quote closes the AppleScript string and the rest is evaluated. Proven
  # executable before this change. Lower trust boundary than a filename on a
  # share - .env is operator-written - but it is the identical class in the
  # identical file, and the header claimed the message was never script text.
  osascript -e 'on run {u}
mount volume u
end run' -- "$SMB_URL" >/dev/null 2>&1
fi

# node is the only binary on this machine with readdir access to the share from
# a launchd context (measured 2026-08-14, see header). If it is missing we
# CANNOT VERIFY, and per this script's founding principle that is an alert.
command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1 || alert DENIED "node not found on PATH - cannot verify the backup (this check requires node; see header)"

# ONE FIELD PER LINE, fixed order, "-" for absent. Values only, never contents.
# A newline in a filename cannot shift this parse - not because filenames
# cannot contain one (on macOS they can), but because the family regexes below
# use `.` and non-multiline anchors, so such a name never matches a family and
# never reaches these fields. See the header for the full reasoning.
#
#   1 global state   OK | UNREACHABLE | DENIED | ERROR
#   2 errcode        the errno name, or "-"
#   then per family, in the order declared below:
#     count, ageHours, sizeBytes, newestName   ("-" when count is 0)
PROBE="$(node -e '
const fs = require("fs");
const dir = process.argv[1];
const families = [
  ["db",      /^careerforge-db-.*\.dump\.age$/],
  ["profile", /^careerforge-profile-.*\.tar\.age$/],
];
const out = [];
let entries;
try {
  entries = fs.readdirSync(dir);
} catch (e) {
  const code = e.code || "UNKNOWN";
  const state = (code === "EPERM" || code === "EACCES") ? "DENIED"
              : (code === "ENOENT" || code === "ENOTDIR") ? "UNREACHABLE"
              : "ERROR";
  process.stdout.write([state, code].join("\n") + "\n");
  process.exit(0);
}
out.push("OK", "-");
for (const [, re] of families) {
  const files = entries.filter((f) => re.test(f));
  if (files.length === 0) { out.push("0", "-", "-", "-"); continue; }
  let newest = "", mtime = 0, size = 0;
  for (const f of files) {
    let st;
    try { st = fs.statSync(dir + "/" + f); } catch { continue; }
    if (st.mtimeMs > mtime) { mtime = st.mtimeMs; newest = f; size = st.size; }
  }
  if (!newest) { out.push(String(files.length), "-", "-", "-"); continue; }
  // Truncation toward zero would turn a small negative age into 0 and hide a
  // future timestamp, so floor() is deliberate: it keeps negatives negative.
  const age = Math.floor((Date.now() - mtime) / 3600000);
  out.push(String(files.length), String(age), String(size), newest);
}
process.stdout.write(out.join("\n") + "\n");
' "$BACKUP_DIR" 2>/dev/null)"

[ -z "$PROBE" ] && alert ERROR "the liveness probe produced no output - cannot verify the backup"

# Command substitution has already stripped the probe's trailing newline, so
# this splits to exactly the fields the probe emitted - no empty tail element
# to trim. (An earlier version trimmed one anyway; the arithmetic in that trim
# was itself a `zsh -n` error, which is a fair summary of why dead defensive
# code is not free.)
FIELDS=("${(@f)PROBE}")

GLOBAL="${FIELDS[1]}"
ERRCODE="${FIELDS[2]}"

case "$GLOBAL" in
  UNREACHABLE)
    # ENOTDIR reaches here too: BACKUP_DIR exists but is a file, not a
    # directory. The wording covers both rather than asserting "unmounted".
    alert UNREACHABLE "cannot read the backup dir ($BACKUP_DIR) - missing, unmounted, or not a directory ($ERRCODE). Cannot verify last night's backup" ;;
  DENIED)
    alert DENIED "CANNOT VERIFY - readdir refused on the backup dir ($ERRCODE). The backup itself may be fine; this check has lost its access. See the header of scripts/launchd/careerforge-backup-liveness." ;;
  ERROR)
    alert ERROR "unexpected error reading the backup dir ($ERRCODE) - cannot verify" ;;
  OK)
    : ;;
  *)
    alert ERROR "unrecognized probe state '$GLOBAL' - cannot verify" ;;
esac

# Four fields per family, starting after the two global fields.
[ "${#FIELDS[@]}" -ne 10 ] && alert ERROR "the liveness probe returned ${#FIELDS[@]} fields, expected 10 - cannot verify"

FAMILY_NAMES=("database dump" "profile archive")
SUMMARY=""
i=3
for name in "${FAMILY_NAMES[@]}"; do
  COUNT="${FIELDS[$i]}"
  AGE="${FIELDS[$((i + 1))]}"
  SIZE="${FIELDS[$((i + 2))]}"
  NEWEST="${FIELDS[$((i + 3))]}"
  i=$((i + 4))

  [ "$COUNT" = "0" ] && alert EMPTY "NO $name artifacts found in the backup dir at all"

  # Guard every numeric BEFORE comparing. An unguarded comparison against a
  # non-numeric value is how (b) above turned into a logged OK.
  #
  # MATCH POSITIVELY. The first version of this guard excluded characters
  # instead - `*[!0-9-]*` - which accepts any string of digits and hyphens.
  # Adversarial review demonstrated the leak: `--`, `1-2`, `-1-1` and `3-4-5`
  # all pass, both comparisons below then error under zsh and evaluate false,
  # and control falls through to `log OK` - reproducing defect (b) inside the
  # guard written to prevent it. Not reachable through the current probe, which
  # emits only `-?\d+`, NaN or Infinity, but a guard whose own escape hatch
  # fails OPEN is the wrong shape for this file.
  [[ "$AGE" =~ '^-?[0-9]+$' ]] \
    || alert ERROR "could not read the age of the newest $name ('$AGE') - cannot verify"
  [[ "$SIZE" =~ '^[0-9]+$' ]] \
    || alert ERROR "could not read the size of the newest $name ('$SIZE') - cannot verify"

  [ "$AGE" -lt 0 ] && alert FUTURE "the newest $name is dated ${AGE#-}h in the FUTURE ($NEWEST) - the clock on this Mac and the one on the backup share disagree, so no age check here can be trusted"
  [ "$SIZE" -eq 0 ] && alert ZEROSIZE "the newest $name is ZERO BYTES ($NEWEST) - the job created the file but wrote nothing to it"
  [ "$AGE" -ge "$MAX_AGE_HOURS" ] && alert STALE "the newest $name is ${AGE}h old (threshold ${MAX_AGE_HOURS}h) - the nightly job has stopped succeeding. Check ~/Library/Logs/careerforge-backup.log"

  # The newest filename stays in the OK line on purpose. It is the only field
  # that would visibly garble if the probe parse ever shifted again, so an
  # operator reading the log can see the parse is intact, and the plant for
  # the delimiter case has something to assert on beyond the exit code.
  SUMMARY="${SUMMARY}${SUMMARY:+; }${name} ${AGE}h old, ${COUNT} present (${NEWEST})"
done

log "OK: $SUMMARY"
exit 0
